this morning we bring you a remarkable  story of hope and perseverance Antonio  Davis was shot 24 years ago in one of  Chicago's roughest neighborhoods it  nearly killed him Davis overcame his  damaging injuries to become a successful  painter he also found love along the way  DeMarco Morgan spoke with him about that  journey DeMarco good morning good  morning Antonio Davis  showed us his life was headed in the  wrong direction when he was shot at  close range back in 1994 what he did  know was that it's life was about to  take an extraordinary turn with purpose  I'm just creating I'm just freeing my  mind and what comes out is my true  emotions through his oil paintings  Antonio Davis brings to life his burning  desire to create his a passion is an  obsession I love it that much and I hope  it shows in the work his work that  includes landscapes still lifes and  portraits but the picture comes from a  different perspective  the 43 old paints with his mouth when  Davis was 19 years old his friend shot  him during a fight he was left paralyzed  from his chest down when you learned  that you were paralyzed do you think  life was over for you at least the way  that you had lived it absolutely I  didn't know anything about people in  wheelchairs I didn't have a  comprehension of the physical  requirements of the daily living with  somebody with their quality of life  so the teenager was placed at our  nursing home to learn how to adjust to  his new wheelchair-bound life and was  there that he met Juanita Butler I  noticed him and I said oh my god you're  so gorgeous what are you doing here he  says I live here oh so she came onto you  yeah she was aggressive very aggressive  very aggressive and she's like I'm gonna  get you out of here she did get him out  of there and a year later they got  married I made the best choice in the  world  when I said I'm getting you out of this  nursing home I found somebody who's so  special who's like a diamond in the  rough and now he's shining  shining through his art something he's  always loved but only pursued after the  incident I try art with my hands using  different devices and how did that work  for you for me it worked fine I thought  that I was doing well but for others it  did an occupational therapist suggested  he tried with his mouth so after some  convincing he did fifteen years later  his paintings are recognized worldwide  and range in price from five to more  than 80 thousand dollars most don't  believe it  because the level that I have gotten to  in my artistry it's where they say you  didn't paint that with your mouth  dave was presented a portrait of former  President Barack Obama to the man  himself what did he think of the picture  that you did for all he loved it the  first response was I love that you  didn't put the gray hair in there so the  artists also gives back he volunteers  his time to teach and inspire art  students on Chicago's South Side at  Richard J Daly Community College I feel  like me going out mouth painting showing  the public the community that you can  have a tragic accident it looks good or  any tragedy that happened to you and you  still can be able to accomplish things  have you ever asked the question why me  no I always dare myself to do better  just do better that's it have you  forgiven the man who shot you  your friend absolutely I have forgave  him and I have forgave the spirit behind  it so his own spirit within could  inspire those around him you just do  what you just do what I do  I just get up and do what I do when  today when two week when the month when  de year when your life  quality certainly has antonio toda said  the shooting was a blessing in disguise  had he not been in the nursing home he  may not have ever met his wife Juanita  and if he wasn't paralyzed and may have  never pursued his passion for it and you  know guys this is a life changing story  for me because it really proves that no  matter the setbacks that come in your  life you can still win and win because I  found love and he's doing what he go  home in sorter to do what was she doing  in the nursing home did she work there  she had a relative there okay so it's an  unbelievable story tomorrow it's one of  those beautiful stories are being told  on the show and we use the phrase and in  the piece the phrase wheelchair-bound  but that looks like freedom to me that  looks like creativity looks like  Tennessee getting higher well you know  what it also proves that happiness and  peace is what matters at the end of the  day so true and the power of people  believing in you and you believing in  yourself do thank you thank you
 

